## The short version

In the lab on {{ S14_DAY }}, {{ S14_DATE }}, every project member runs the full **maintainer-centered loop** on your project's real repo — issue → branch → logical commits → PR with a real description → review another maintainer's PR → respond to review → merge under policy — plus one staged **merge conflict**, resolved. Each project finishes by writing its **git standards** into the repo. Your grade is read directly from the repo: its issues, pull requests, reviews, and history. The <course-link type="page" id="pg-git-lab-guide">Git Workflow Lab Guide</course-link> walks every step, with the commands.

---

## Why this assignment exists

You've used git; this lab is about *running* git — the difference between knowing the commands and operating the collaboration system a real project lives on. From next week until December, every feature your project ships travels through this loop, and the quality of your project's history — its commits, PRs, and reviews — becomes the public record of your engineering. A future contributor (and the Week-11 auditor, and any hiring manager who opens your repo) reads that record the way you'd read someone's prose: it shows whether a developer is a good collaborator.

Three specific habits are being installed. **Commit messages that explain why** — because a diff shows *what* changed, and only the message preserves the reasoning your future self will need. **PRs sized and described to be reviewable** — because review is where teams catch design drift, and an unreviewable PR silently disables it. **Written code review done kindly and substantively** — comment on the code, not the coder; a skill that shapes team culture more than almost any other. The lab makes the content trivial *on purpose*: with nothing hard to build, all attention goes to the process — which is the thing being learned.

---

## What to produce (all in the repo, by end of day {{ S14_DAY }})

Per **member**:
1. An **issue** for your starter task, filed before you start the work, and a branch (named per your convention) with **1–2 logical commits** whose messages pass the seven rules (subject ≤ ~50 chars, imperative mood, blank line, body explains *what/why*).
2. A **PR** with a real description (problem · approach · what to look at first) that links and closes the issue.
3. A **written review** of another maintainer's PR — a teammate's, or on a solo project, your review partner's: at least two comments, one substantive.
4. **Responses to every comment** received (fix or explain), and a merge under the project's policy. *(Whatever policy your project sets later, the lab itself requires ≥1 approval from someone who didn't write the change — the review is part of what's graded.)*

Per **project**:
5. One **staged merge conflict** created and correctly resolved, by merging `main` into the branch, so the resolving merge commit is in that PR. *(Solo: open both conflicting branches yourself; your review partner reviews the PR that resolves it.)*
6. **Git standards** written and merged (e.g., `docs/git-standards.md`): branch naming · commit convention · PR size norms · review requirements · merge strategy · definition of "done" — each with a line of rationale.

---

## Requirements & constraints

- **Everyone drives.** Each member's own branch, PR, review, and responses — this is individually visible by design.
- **The starter tasks are trivial on purpose** (README additions, doc stubs, typo fixes). Don't gold-plate them.
- **The pull requests are the submission.** Squash merging is fine (it's the suggested default): the branch commits, the review, and the conflict resolution all stay in each PR, which is where grading reads them. What destroys the evidence is rewriting a branch *before* it merges: squashing or rebasing locally and force-pushing over commits your reviewer read, or over the conflict resolution. Don't.
- **Working solo?** You run the same loop. Your review partner, another solo founder, reviews your PR, and you review theirs.

